:ABS
ABS(n)
  Returns n without its sign.
  PRINT ABS(-3)
:AND
a AND b
  Bitwise AND of two numbers from -32768 to 32767. True is -1, false 0.
  IF A>0 AND B>0 THEN PRINT "BOTH"
  PRINT 12 AND 10
:ASC
ASC(a$)
  Returns the code of the first character of a$.
  PRINT ASC("A")
:ATN
ATN(n)
  Returns the arctangent of n, in radians.
:BIN$
BIN$(n)
  Returns n in binary: 8 digits below 256, else 16. n is -65535 to 65535;
  a negative n is taken mod 65536.
  PRINT BIN$(5)
:CHR$
CHR$(code)
  Returns the character of code, 0 to 255.
  PRINT CHR$(65)
:CLEAR
CLEAR
  Clears all variables, arrays and strings, and closes the open files.
:CLOSE
CLOSE lf
  Closes logical file lf. A file that is not open is ignored. If it was the
  input or output, the console takes over.
  CLOSE 1
:CLS
CLS
  Clears the screen and puts the cursor at the top left.
:CMD
CMD lf
  Sends the output of PRINT to logical file lf instead of the screen.
  PRINT# lf ends it.
  CMD 1: LIST
:COLOR
COLOR fg[,bg]
  Sets the text color and the background, 0 to 15 each. Without bg the
  background stays.
  COLOR 1,6
:CONT
CONT
  Goes on with the program after STOP, END or a break.
:CONTINUE
CONTINUE
  Starts the next pass of the innermost FOR or DO loop.
  10 FOR I=1 TO 5: IF I=3 THEN CONTINUE
  20 PRINT I: NEXT
:COS
COS(n)
  Returns the cosine of n, n in radians.
:DATA
DATA value[,value...]
  Holds numbers and strings for READ.
  DATA 1,2,"THREE"
:DEEK
DEEK(addr)
  Returns the 16-bit word at addr, low byte first, 0 to 65535.
  PRINT DEEK(2)
:DEF
DEF FN name(x) = expression                         (Program only)
  Defines a function of one number.
  10 DEF FN SQ(X) = X*X
  20 PRINT FN SQ(4)
:DIM
DIM name(size[,size...])[,...]
  Makes arrays. Each index runs from 0 to size. An array used without DIM
  has 0 to 10.
  DIM A(20), N$(5,5)
:DO
DO ... LOOP
  Repeats the lines between DO and LOOP. EXIT leaves the loop. CONTINUE
  starts the next pass.
  10 DO
  20 INPUT A
  30 IF A=0 THEN EXIT
  40 PRINT A*2
  50 LOOP
:DOKE
DOKE addr,n
  Stores n as a 16-bit word at addr, low byte first. n is -65535 to 65535;
  a negative n is taken mod 65536.
:ELSE
ELSE
  Starts the part of a block IF that runs when the condition is false.
  ELSE IF c THEN chains blocks; each block needs its own END IF.
  See IF.
:END
END
  Ends the program. END IF ends a block IF.
:EXIT
EXIT [DO]
  Leaves a DO loop and goes on after its LOOP.
:EXP
EXP(n)
  Returns e to the power n.
:FN
FN name(x)
  Calls a function that DEF FN made. See DEF.
:FOR
FOR var = first TO last [STEP n] ... NEXT [var]
  Runs the lines between FOR and NEXT once for each value of var.
  STEP sets the change after each pass; it is 1 without STEP.
  FOR I=10 TO 1 STEP -1: PRINT I: NEXT
:FRE
FRE(0)
  Returns the free bytes of memory. Over 32767 it is negative; add 65536.
:GET
GET var[,var...]                                    (Program only)
GET# lf,var[,var...]
  Reads one character from the input, or from logical file lf.
  10 GET K$
:GO
GO TO line
  The same as GOTO.
:GOSUB
GOSUB line or label
  Runs the subroutine at line or label. RETURN comes back after GOSUB.
  GOSUB 1000
:GOTO
GOTO line or label
  Goes on at line or label.
  GOTO 100
  GOTO MAIN
:HELP
HELP [topic]                                        (Direct only)
  Prints the help on topic, or the topic names, from C:\DOS\HELPBAS.HLP.
  The shell command HELPBAS does the same.
  HELP PRINT
:HEX$
HEX$(n)
  Returns n in hex: 2 digits below 256, else 4. n is -65535 to 65535;
  a negative n is taken mod 65536.
  PRINT HEX$(255)
:IF
IF c THEN statements
IF c THEN line
IF c THEN ... [ELSE ...] END IF
  Runs the statements, or goes to line, when c is true (not 0).
  A line that ends with THEN starts a block IF, which ends at END IF.
  10 IF A=1 THEN
  20   PRINT "ONE"
  30 ELSE
  40   PRINT "OTHER"
  50 END IF
:INPUT
INPUT ["prompt";] var[,var...]                      (Program only)
  Reads values that the user types, separated by commas.
  10 INPUT "NAME";N$
:INPUT#
INPUT# lf,var[,var...]
  Reads values from logical file lf.
  INPUT# 1,A$
:INSTR
INSTR([start,] a$, b$)
  Returns the position of b$ in a$ from start on, or 0.
  PRINT INSTR("HELLO","L")
:INT
INT(n)
  Returns the largest whole number not above n.
  PRINT INT(-2.5)
:LABELS
name:
  A name and a colon at the start of a line make a label. GOTO, GOSUB,
  RUN, RESTORE and ON take the label in place of a line number.
  10 GOSUB SHOW: END
  20 SHOW: PRINT "HI": RETURN
:LCASE$
LCASE$(a$)
  Returns a$ with the letters A to Z made small.
:LEFT$
LEFT$(a$,n)
  Returns the first n characters of a$.
  PRINT LEFT$("HELLO",2)
:LEN
LEN(a$)
  Returns the number of characters in a$.
:LIST
LIST [first][-[last]]
  Lists the program, or the lines from first to last.
  LIST 100-200
:LOCATE
LOCATE x,y
  Moves the cursor to column x, row y. 0,0 is the top left.
  LOCATE 10,5: PRINT "HERE"
:LOG
LOG(n)
  Returns the natural logarithm of n.
:LOOP
LOOP
  Ends a DO loop. See DO.
:MID$
MID$(a$,start[,n])
  Returns n characters of a$ from start on, or all of the rest.
  PRINT MID$("HELLO",2,3)
:MOD
a MOD b
  Returns the remainder of a / b, with the sign of a. Both are rounded to
  whole numbers first.
  PRINT 7 MOD 3
:NEW
NEW
  Deletes the program and its variables.
:NEXT
NEXT [var[,var...]]
  Ends a FOR loop. See FOR.
:NOT
NOT n
  Bitwise NOT of a number from -32768 to 32767. NOT 0 is -1.
:ON
ON n GOTO line[,line...]
ON n GOSUB line[,line...]
  Goes to the nth line of the list. Labels work as well.
  ON K GOTO 100,200,300
:OPEN
OPEN lf,"name"[,mode]
  Opens a file as logical file lf, 1 to 255. Mode 0 reads, 1 writes,
  2 appends. Writing makes the file or empties it.
  OPEN 1,"DATA.TXT",1
:OR
a OR b
  Bitwise OR of two numbers from -32768 to 32767. True is -1, false 0.
:PAUSE
PAUSE n
  Waits n+1 jiffies, 1/60 s each. PAUSE 60 waits about a second.
:PEEK
PEEK(addr)
  Returns the byte at addr.
:PI
PI
  The number pi, 3.14159265.
:POKE
POKE addr,n
  Stores the byte n at addr.
:POS
POS(n)
  POS(0) returns the cursor's column. POS(1) returns its row.
:PRINT
PRINT [items]
  Prints numbers and strings. A ; joins items, a , moves to the next zone
  of 14 columns. A ; or , at the end keeps the cursor on the line.
  PRINT "A=";A
:PRINT#
PRINT# lf[,items]
  Prints to logical file lf.
  PRINT# 1,"LINE"
:QUIT
QUIT [n]
  Ends BASIC and returns to the shell, with exit code n, 0 to 255, or 0.
:READ
READ var[,var...]
  Reads the next values from the DATA lines.
:REM
REM text
  A comment. The rest of the line is skipped.
:RESTORE
RESTORE [line or label]
  Makes READ start again at the first DATA, or at line or label.
:RETURN
RETURN
  Returns from GOSUB.
:RIGHT$
RIGHT$(a$,n)
  Returns the last n characters of a$.
:RND
RND(n)
  RND(1) returns a random number from 0 to below 1. A negative n starts a
  new series.
  PRINT INT(RND(1)*6)+1
:RPT$
RPT$(a$,n)
RPT$(code,n)
  Returns a$, or CHR$(code), n times, n 0 to 255.
  PRINT RPT$("-",40)
:RUN
RUN [line or label]
  Clears the variables and runs the program, from the start or from line.
:SCREEN
SCREEN mode
  Does nothing. The screen stays 80 by 30 text.
:SGN
SGN(n)
  Returns 1 for a positive n, -1 for a negative n, 0 for 0.
:SHL
SHL(a,n)
  Shifts the 16 bits of a left n places. The result is -32768 to 32767.
  PRINT SHL(1,4)
:SHR
SHR(a,n)
  Shifts the 16 bits of a right n places. Zeros come in at the top.
:SIN
SIN(n)
  Returns the sine of n, n in radians.
:SQR
SQR(n)
  Returns the square root of n.
:ST
ST
  The status of the last file read. 64 means the end of the file.
  IF ST AND 64 THEN CLOSE 1
:STEP
STEP n
  Sets the change of a FOR loop. See FOR.
:STOP
STOP
  Stops the program with BREAK IN line. CONT goes on.
:STR$
STR$(n)
  Returns n as a string.
:SYS
SYS addr
  Calls machine code at addr. RTS returns to BASIC.
:TAB
TAB(n)
  In PRINT, moves to column n.
  PRINT TAB(10);"X"
:TAN
TAN(n)
  Returns the tangent of n, n in radians.
:TEXTAT
TEXTAT x,y,a$[,color]
TEXTAT x,y,code[,color]
  Prints a$, or CHR$(code), at column x, row y, in color if given. The
  cursor stays where it was.
  TEXTAT 0,0,"SCORE",14
:THEN
THEN
  Follows the condition of IF. See IF.
:TO
TO
  Sets the last value of a FOR loop. See FOR.
:UCASE$
UCASE$(a$)
  Returns a$ with the letters a to z made capital.
:VAL
VAL(a$)
  Returns the number in a$, or 0.
  PRINT VAL("12")+1
:VPEEK
VPEEK(bank,addr)
  Returns the byte at addr in video memory bank, 0 or 1.
:VPOKE
VPOKE bank,addr,n
  Stores the byte n at addr in video memory bank, 0 or 1.
:WAIT
WAIT addr,and[,xor]
  Waits until (PEEK(addr) XOR xor) AND and is not 0.
:XOR
XOR(a,b)
  Bitwise XOR of the 16 bits of a and b. The result is -32768 to 32767.
  PRINT XOR(12,10)
